### 3. [`src/schema/businessEntity.ts`](file:///c:/Users/ASUS/my-agentic-cdp/src/schema/businessEntity.ts) — The Core Data Model

```typescript
export type RiskLevel = 'LOW' | 'MEDIUM' | 'HIGH' | 'CRITICAL';

export interface BusinessEntity {
  id: string;                              // Canonical unique ID (gst:xxx, pan:xxx, or party:xxx)
  name: string;                            // Human-readable business name
  ledgerName: string;                      // Name as it appears in accounting ledger
  taxId?: string | undefined;              // GSTIN (optional)
  currentOutstanding?: number | undefined;  // Amount owed (filled later from outstanding ledger)
  riskLevel?: RiskLevel | undefined;        // Risk classification (filled later by ageing pipeline)
}
```

**This is the single source of truth for what a "business" looks like in this system.**

| Field | Source | When It's Set |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | Generated by `mapper.ts` | During Party Master parsing |
| `name` | From `party_master.xls` column `name` | During Party Master parsing |
| `ledgerName` | From `party_master.xls` column `ledger` | During Party Master parsing |
| `taxId` | From `party_master.xls` column `tin` (GSTIN) | During Party Master parsing |
| `currentOutstanding` | From `outstanding_legder.xls` column `Total` | During ingestion (Step 3) |
| `riskLevel` | Computed by `ageing.ts` | During ingestion (Step 3) |

**Key Learning**: Notice the `?:  string | undefined` pattern. With `exactOptionalPropertyTypes: true` in tsconfig, writing `taxId?: string` alone would mean "the property can be missing but NOT explicitly `undefined`". Adding `| undefined` allows both.

**Key Learning**: `RiskLevel` is a **Union Type** — it can only be one of the four literal strings. This is TypeScript's way of creating an enum-like constraint without using the `enum` keyword.

