### 7. [`src/identity/resolver.ts`](file:///c:/Users/ASUS/my-agentic-cdp/src/identity/resolver.ts) — Identity Resolution Engine

This is the **brain** of the CDP. It answers: *"Given a row from the outstanding ledger, which business from the party master does it belong to?"*

#### The Result Type

```typescript
export type ResolutionResult =
    | { status: 'NOT_FOUND' }                                              // No match at all
    | { status: 'FOUND'; business: BusinessEntity; matchType: string }     // Exactly one match
    | { status: 'AMBIGUOUS'; candidates: BusinessEntity[]; matchType: string }; // Multiple matches
```

This is a **Discriminated Union** — the `status` field tells TypeScript which shape the object has. When you check `if (result.status === 'FOUND')`, TypeScript knows `result.business` exists in that branch.

#### Function 1: `findBusinessByLedgerName()` — Name-Based Matching

```typescript
export function findBusinessByLedgerName(
    description: string,
    allBusinesses: BusinessEntity[]
): ResolutionResult {
    const normalized = description.trim();
    const matches = allBusinesses.filter(b => b.ledgerName.trim() === normalized);

    if (matches.length === 0)  return { status: 'NOT_FOUND' };
    if (matches.length === 1)  return { status: 'FOUND', business: matches[0]!, matchType: 'LEDGER_NAME' };
    return { status: 'AMBIGUOUS', candidates: matches, matchType: 'LEDGER_NAME' };
}
```

Simple **exact string match** on ledger name. Trims whitespace but does NOT do fuzzy matching.

#### Function 2: `resolveBusiness()` — The Main Resolver (GSTIN-First Strategy)

```typescript
export function resolveBusiness(
    outstandingRow: { description: string; gstin?: string },
    allBusinesses: BusinessEntity[]
): ResolutionResult {

    // ATTEMPT 1: Match by GSTIN (strongest identifier)
    if (outstandingRow.gstin) {
        const gstinMatches = allBusinesses.filter(b => b.taxId === outstandingRow.gstin);

        if (gstinMatches.length === 1) return { status: 'FOUND', ... matchType: 'CANONICAL_ID' };
        if (gstinMatches.length > 1) return { status: 'AMBIGUOUS', ... };
        // length === 0: NO return — falls through to name-based matching
    }

    // ATTEMPT 2: Fallback to name matching
    return findBusinessByLedgerName(outstandingRow.description, allBusinesses);
}
```

**Resolution Priority**:
```
Outstanding Row comes in →
  Has GSTIN?  →  YES → Match by GSTIN in party master
                        → 1 match = FOUND
                        → 2+ matches = AMBIGUOUS
                        → 0 matches = fall through ↓
              →  NO  ↓
  Match by ledger name ← 
    → 1 match = FOUND
    → 2+ matches = AMBIGUOUS
    → 0 matches = NOT_FOUND
```

**Key Learnings**:
- **Identity Resolution** is a core CDP concept — in the real world, the same customer might appear with different names, different IDs, or across different systems. The resolver's job is to figure out "who is who".
- The **fallthrough pattern** (no `return` when GSTIN matches 0 records) is deliberate — if the GSTIN isn't found, maybe the outstanding ledger row just doesn't have GSTIN but we can still match by name.
- `matchType` tracks HOW the match was made — useful for auditing and debugging.

